<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Need Fewer Moving Boxes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Declutter first.</strong> Every item you donate is one you do not have to wrap, carry and unpack. Start with closets using our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">closet clean-out checklist</a>.</li>


<li><strong>Pack soft things in what you already own.</strong> Suitcases, duffel bags and laundry baskets hold clothes, towels and bedding.</li>


<li><strong>Leave light items in drawers.</strong> Many movers let you leave clothes in dresser drawers if the dresser is not too heavy to carry. Ask your mover first.</li>


<li><strong>Use wardrobe boxes for hanging clothes.</strong> They hold clothes on their hangers, so you skip folding and the closet is ready to fill on day one.</li>


<li><strong>Eat down the pantry and freezer.</strong> Plan meals for the last two weeks so there is less food to pack.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once you arrive, unpack the kitchen and bedrooms first. Our guides on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-organize-kitchen-cabinets/">how to organize kitchen cabinets</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a> help you set up the new place, and our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a> are useful if you are moving somewhere smaller.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Moving Box FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many boxes do I need for a 1-bedroom apartment?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Plan on about 30 to 40 boxes for an average 1-bedroom apartment, or 20 to 30 if you own very little. That is usually around 12 small, 13 medium, 9 large or extra-large, 2 wardrobe boxes and 1 dish pack.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many boxes do I need for a 2-bedroom apartment?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An average 2-bedroom apartment needs about 50 to 60 boxes. Light packers can manage with about 40, and a home with lots of books, toys or a storage unit can need 70 or more.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many boxes do I need for a 3-bedroom house?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A 3-bedroom house with a garage typically needs about 80 to 100 boxes. Garages, basements and attics vary the most, so look inside them before you buy.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many wardrobe boxes do I need?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A common rule of thumb from <a href="https://www.movingplace.com/moving-advice/how-many-moving-boxes-do-i-need" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Moving Place</a> is about one wardrobe box for every 2 feet (60 cm) of closet rail. Fold what you can into regular boxes and keep wardrobe boxes for shirts, dresses and coats that crease.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Where can I get moving boxes?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can buy new boxes from moving truck rental companies, home improvement stores and online, or look for used boxes through local buy-and-sell groups and neighbors who just moved. Check used boxes for damp, crushed corners and pests before you pack them.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">More Planning Tools</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Planning the new place? Try our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/curtain-size-calculator/">curtain size calculator</a> for new windows, the <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/washer-dryer-space-calculator/">washer and dryer space calculator</a> to check that your machines fit, and the <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/shelf-spacing-calculator/">shelf spacing calculator</a> for closets and pantries.</p>



